\f3\fs26 Shakti was sucking in big breaths, his nerves were always shot just before going to air and on this occasion he knew the show could really go off the rails. The sponsors would be none too pleased if ratings fell again. He had been in the edit-suite with one of his minions, tidying up the profile on one of the human offerings for the evening show. There was not much evidence on this guy, he might have said he bombed the nightclub, but it could have been something else, the microphone did not quite pick it up. The magic of the post-production, however, allowed Shakti to clear up the ambiguity by dubbing in some really damning evidence. \
Shakti was not terribly happy with embellishing the evidence to ensure a conviction, but he was under real pressure lately to get the viewers back above 2 million. Below that mark the revenues were still reasonable, but once you crossed the 2 million barrier, the sponsors throw money at you. Shakti found himself in a compromising position for the second time in as many days. Cracker had really helped him out at the public hygiene unit, but he was concerned about when and in what form he would have to pay back the favour. He could handle forking out some cash, in fact, that was the preferred option, but from the sound of it, Cracker will require something entirely different and no doubt, unethical. \
\'93Good evening my friends. Welcome to a very special and solemn episode of Random Camera. We were all shocked to our core by the cowardly bombing of the Stick-it-Inn and we remain frustrated by the lack of action by the authorities. I, myself, made the only arrest on the night of the bombing, but he died before interrogation.\'94 He paused, waiting for the canned applause to fire up the studio audience. \'93Tonight, right here on Random Camera, we will be bringing the first live offender to justice.\'94 He raised his arms high above his head and was met with a wall of excited acclamation. \'93He a devious, level one scoundrel and a tradesman. Ladies and Gentlemen of the Management Class, please revile John Hammersmith.\'94\
A poor emaciated looking sod was dragged out by two giant, well lubricated body builder types, who with contrived pomp and ceremony dumped the poor bastard on the studio floor, at the feet of the Shakti. \
\'93You have the wrong man. I haven\'92t done anything.\'94 His pathetic petition only caused the crowd to laugh loudly, a few booing the doomed prisoner. \
Shakti smugly grinned directly into the camera, \'93That\'92s what they all say.\'94 The crowd laughed even louder. \'93We shall see. Roll the file.\'94\
The giant screen behind Shakti burst into life. The surveillance footage was grainy, deliberately so, to give Mr Hammersmith an air of suspicion. It followed him on his daily journey, digging trenches and gluing pipes together, laughing mindlessly with his dim-witted colleagues, each grab ending with a close-up of John\'92s evil face. At bars and in homes everywhere, level 4\'92s and 5\'92s were laughing and giggling with delight.\
\'93Awe, yuck! Did you see that? He touched the dirt with his hands! Disgusting.\'94 An audience member was heard to say as she elbowed her neighbour.\
\'93He looks so guilty.\'94 She replied.\
The 15 minute hatchet-job reached its height in the final grab when, out of the blue, John Hammersmith was heard to say in a clear loud voice, \'93I bombed the Stick It Inn. I had to avenge my wife.\'94 The crowd went wild, screaming and booing. \
\'93Guilty!\'94 Was called out from the back of the crowd.\
\'93He\'92s guilty. Death to the terrorist! Death, death.\'94 The crowd followed and continued to chant, \'93Death, death, death\'85..\'94\
The \'93Quiet Please\'94 sign above the studio floor illuminated and the crowd settled quickly to a rumble of murmurs and disquiet. \
\'93What have you to say now, John Hammersmith?\'94 Shakti read the autocue word for word.\
\'93I don\'92t have a wife, I have never been married.\'94 He pleaded. \'93I was in hospital on the night of the explosion.\'94\
\'93He doesn\'92t have a wife and he was sick. Ohhh.\'94 The auto-cue instructed him to deliver with a tone of sarcasm and he did a half-decent job. \'93Well John, thank you for your input, but it\'92s time for the verdict!\'94 \
The studio audience, now more accurately described as a mob, anxious to deal out some justice, raised their hands, waiting for the signal to bash to guilty button. Their faces taking on manic, wide eyed, Nuremburg-esque masks.\
\'93We all know how to vote by now, but for those who may be tuning in for the first time, here are the rules. Each home viewer has one vote and can cast it on the link, by communicator or, for those with holoscreens, grab the red ball on the top right and follow the prompts. The lucky members of the audience have 5,000 votes each. Please vote now. We will accept votes for the next five minutes.\'94\
Votes poured in from every part of the Dominion, with zero votes for not guilty. No-one cared that he had an alibi or no wife to avenge, he was guilty of being working class. He touched the unclean earth with his bare hands. He was sickening example of what was wrong with the world and had to be put down. The victims of the night club bombing would have their justice.\
\'93The votes are in my friends.\'94 Shakti announced and the crowd became hushed with anticipation. \'93We have received 2.17 million votes. I think there might be some people out there voting more than once, but that\'92s okay. Vote early, vote often we say.\'94 The crowd laughed heartily. They loved autocue jokes. \'932.17 million votes for guilty, zero not guilty.\'94 The autocue told him to be silent and there was silence for the longest time. \'93The people have spoken, Mr Hammersmith. You have been found guilty.\'94\
The crowd went nuts. Jumping up and down in their little places.\
\'93Mr Hammersmith and will now be sentenced. We only have two sentences available on this show, Death or Pardon. So what will it be people? Death or Pardon? We will be taking votes for the next five minutes\'94 Shakti stared down the camera, sparkling a fake on-camera grin as the shot faded and advertisements took his place.\
\'93Thanks for those important messages from our sponsors.\'94 Shakti parroted on his return to the screen. \'93Remember for all your family planning needs, contact Pattinson\'92s Mobile Abortions. They come to you and take your worries away. Just call 1800-2229-4663. That\'92s 1800 BABY-GONE.\'94 \
The crowd clapped and screamed loudly, the studio taking on a carnival atmosphere. \'93Wow! Is this a great crowd or what?\'94 They yelled and clapped louder. \'93Let\'92s see what punishment has been meted out tonight. There are no votes for a pardon so Mr Hammersmith you have been sentenced to death. Now the only matter to decide is the manner of your execution. Bring out the wheel of death.\'94 \
\'93Wheel of Death, Wheel of Death\'94 The audience was chanting in full voice.\
A large Chocolate Wheel was brought out by a pretty young girl in a cliched, but elaborate head-dress and swimsuit. \'93Now as you can see there are 10 possible methods of execution. Let\'92s see what they are tonight. Number 1, Boiling in oil. Number 2, Trebuchet Slap. Number 3, Clubbed to death by Dwarves, a crowd favourite. Number 4, Shark Tank. Number 5, One Thousand cuts. Number 6, Acid Injection to the Brain. Number 7, Snake Pit. Number 8, Cesspool Drowning. Number 9, Packet of Peanuts. Ooh, allergic are we? Number 10, Helium Chamber. What do you think of those options?\'94\
\'93Oh, someone help me! I haven\'92t done a thing.\'94 He was shaking uncontrollable and spit-froth had appeared at the corners of his mouth.\
\'93Well, C\'92mon. Spin the Wheel!\'94\
\'93Spin the wheel, spin the wheel, spin the wheel.\'94 They were now stomping there feet in a slow cadence. \
The shivering man approached the wheel, but could not raise his hands. Two of the oiled body-builders shuffled him in position, placed his hand on the wheel and brought it down, spinning the wheel to a blur.\
\'93Number 2. Trebuchet Slap!\'94 Shakti raised his hands to the crowd and they responded in wild exaltation. \
The huge curtains at the rear of the set opened to reveal a tall and sturdy wood framed siege engine, replete with hulking iron brackets, giant round head nails, ropes and pulleys. The A-frame structure sat atop a set of solid wooden wheels. A long thick beam, about 6 metres long, was held at the apex of the frame at a pivot point, about two thirds along its length. Swinging from the short end of the beam was a large counter weight and a lengthy sling and pouch hung low from the far end. The pleading and screaming commenced as the condemned man was dragged over and strapped into the payload pouch. The Giant counter-weight was winched into place and the ratchet held it firmly. A drum roll built up, drowning out the wretched pleas. The audience became silent, eyes wide, clenched fists and clammy hands were wiped across thighs, adrenalin surged raising heart rates, escalating blood pressure. Short sharp breaths were snatched from the electric air and hair at the back of the all necks stood erect. \
As the drum roll reached fever pitch the swimsuit model released the ratchet and the giant weight fell towards to floor causing the far end of the beam to pivot up quickly, the condemned screaming man was dragged up by the sling and pouch and slapped into the floor with a sickening thud. He did not die, but moaned loudly until some playful music began and 6 dwarves armed with baseball bats marched onto the stage.\
\'93Finishing him off!\'94 The crowd yelled in synchronicity.\
The dwarves descended upon the moaning bag of shattered bones and laid into him with rage and ferocity. The audience was now pointing and laughing hysterically. After a good clubbing he was declared dead and the crowd leapt to their feet clapping. Shakti stood centre stage and soaked up the adulation as slow motion replays of the execution were replayed over and over again on giant screens behind him.\

